@page "/docs/base"
<PageTitle>Blaizio.Base - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Blaizio.Base</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">NuGet</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">headless</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">zero CSS</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p>The headless layer every styled component is built on, and a complete toolkit on its own. Each <code>Base*</code> primitive renders the right element with the right ARIA roles, keyboard behavior, focus management and <code>data-state</code> attributes, and ships <b>not one line of CSS</b>. The styled <code>Bz*</code> components the CLI copies into your project are thin Tailwind layers over them, so for your own design system you can skip those and style Base directly.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Install &amp; register</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/Install" />
        <p>One DI call registers everything the package needs: the imperative <a href="docs/dialog-service">dialog</a> and <a href="docs/components/toast">toast</a> services with their scoped stores, the <code>IThemeService</code>, and the app-wide defaults. Scoped means one instance per circuit on Blazor Server and one per app on WebAssembly. Calling <code>AddBlaizio()</code> twice is safe, because the first registration wins.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/Register" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Anatomy of a primitive</h2>
        <p>Primitives compose. A stateful root cascades its context to the parts inside it, and every part exposes its state as <b>attributes</b> rather than styles. Three ideas cover the whole library:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Composition.</b> A root owns the state (<code>BaseCollapsible</code>) and its parts read it (<code>BaseCollapsibleTrigger</code>, <code>BaseCollapsibleContent</code>). There is no configuration object, because the markup <em>is</em> the configuration.</li>
            <li><b>The <code>data-state</code> contract.</b> Parts carry attributes such as <code>data-state="open"</code>, <code>data-disabled</code> and <code>data-side="top"</code>, and your stylesheet targets them with whatever you use: Tailwind variants, plain CSS or CSS modules.</li>
            <li><b>Accessibility built in.</b> Roles, <code>aria-*</code> wiring, ids, focus and keyboard handling are the primitive's job, not yours.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/Collapsible" />
        <p>Then style those attributes. The collapsible also measures its panel into <code>--bz-collapsible-content-height</code> and keeps the value current while the panel is open, so a height animation takes a single pair of keyframes:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/CollapsibleCss" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">RenderAs: your element, our behavior</h2>
        <p>Every interactive part accepts a <code>RenderAs</code> template. Instead of the primitive's default element, you render <em>your own</em> and apply the provided <code>Attributes</code> to it with <code>@@attributes</code>, which carries over the ARIA attributes, <code>data-*</code> attributes and event handlers. The styled layer uses this to style the primitives, and you can use it to add Blaizio behavior to an existing design system:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/RenderAs" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Controlled or uncontrolled: your choice</h2>
        <p>Stateful primitives work both ways. Each instance decides based on the parameters you pass:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Uncontrolled.</b> Pass nothing, or a <code>Default*</code> starting value, and the primitive keeps its own state.</li>
            <li><b>Controlled.</b> Bind the state (<code>@@bind-Open</code>, <code>@@bind-Value</code> and so on) and you own it, so you can persist it, sync it or change it from anywhere. The primitive never changes a controlled value itself. It raises the change, and your new value flows back in.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/Controlled" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Renderless utilities</h2>
        <p>Some primitives render no markup of their own and exist only for their behavior:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Primitive</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>What it does</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>BaseHotkey</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A global keyboard shortcut: <code>Combo="mod+k"</code> invokes a callback anywhere on the page. Bare keys are suppressed while typing in inputs.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>BaseFocusScope</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Traps focus inside a region and restores it afterwards. Dialogs and menus use it, and it is available for your own overlays.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>BaseDirectionProvider</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Cascades a reading direction (<code>Ltr</code> or <code>Rtl</code>) to everything inside it. See <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/direction">Direction</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>BaseRovingFocusGroup</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Arrow-key focus movement across a set of items (toolbars, radio groups, menus).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>BaseVisuallyHidden</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Content for screen readers only: it is rendered and announced, but not visible.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/Hotkey" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Services</h2>
        <p>Four imperative APIs come with the package, all registered by <code>AddBlaizio()</code>:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b><code>ICore</code></b> covers the browser-side pieces Blazor cannot provide reliably from C#. <code>FocusAsync</code>, by reference or id, retries across animation frames until the element can actually take focus, so focusing something during an opening animation works where the built-in <code>ElementReference.FocusAsync</code> silently fails. <code>EnsureGuardsAsync</code> installs the page-level handlers behind <code>data-bz-prevent-keys</code> (<code>preventDefault</code> for specific key combinations), <code>data-bz-autoselect</code> and <code>data-bz-autofocus</code>.</li>
            <li><b><code>IDialogService</code></b> opens any component as a modal and lets you await its result, with shortcuts for confirm and alert dialogs. The full guide is <a href="docs/dialog-service">Dialog Service</a>.</li>
            <li><b><code>IToastService</code></b> shows notifications without waiting on them, including toasts that track a loading task until it finishes. See <a href="docs/components/toast">Toast</a>.</li>
            <li><b><code>IThemeService</code></b> gives you control in code over everything the theme switcher touches: light or dark mode, style, color preset, direction, chart palette, radius and fonts. Changes are persisted and kept in sync with every switcher on the page.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/Core" />
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Base/ThemeService" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">The primitive catalog</h2>
        <p>Every styled component's behavior lives here, and each component page documents the behavior of both layers. The families are:</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
            @foreach (var family in Families)
            {
                <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Base@(family)</BzBadge>
            }
        </div>
        <p>The package also includes the utilities above and the shared infrastructure (<code>BasePrimitive</code>, <code>PropBuilder</code>, <code>ControllableState&lt;T&gt;</code>) for writing primitives of your own the same way.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Base and the styled layer</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Start <b>Base-only</b> and build your own look. Nothing else is required.</li>
            <li>Or run <a href="docs/cli">the CLI</a> to get the styled components as <b>source you own</b>. Each one is a worked example of styling these primitives.</li>
            <li>Mix the two freely: styled components where they fit, raw primitives where your design differs. The behavior and accessibility are the same either way.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly string[] Families =
    [
        "Accordion", "AspectRatio", "Attachment", "Avatar", "Calendar", "Carousel", "Checkbox",
        "Collapsible", "Combobox", "Command", "ContextMenu", "Dialog", "DropdownMenu", "HoverCard",
        "Image", "InputNumber", "InputOtp", "InputTags", "Menubar", "NavigationMenu", "Popover",
        "Progress", "RadioGroup", "Resizable", "ScrollArea", "Select", "Separator", "Slider",
        "Sortable", "Switch", "Table", "TableOfContents", "Tabs", "Toast", "Toggle", "ToggleGroup",
        "Tooltip", "Tree",
    ];
}
